EOF
            ;;
        status)
            cat <<'EOF'
status — project overview + deploy readiness.
  Shows git branch, roadmap completion, exploit methods, offset
  coverage, and a plain-English readiness checklist: do you have a
  device, is it online, are build tools present, are offsets covered.
EOF
            ;;
        monitor)
            cat <<'EOF'
monitor — watch the phone's tweak log live.
  Tails /tmp/FilzaTweak.log over SSH and color-codes each line:
  green = success/escaped, red = errors, yellow = retries.
  Ctrl+C to stop.
EOF
            ;;
        log)
            cat <<'EOF'
log — fetch the phone's tweak log.
  Pulls the last N lines of /tmp/FilzaTweak.log over SSH. This is the
  first place to look when the exploit didn't escape.
EOF
            ;;
        reboot)
            cat <<'EOF'
reboot — restart the phone over SSH.
  Remember: a reboot wipes the exploit. It runs again fresh the next
  time Filza opens. Also the rescue button if the phone ever wedges.
EOF
            ;;
        offsets)
            cat <<'EOF'
offsets — which iOS versions this exploit supports.
  Lists every iOS version block in kexploit/offsets.m and the SoC
  coverage. If your iOS version is missing, the exploit cannot run
  safely — see docs/OFFSET_RESOLUTION_GUIDE.md for adding it.
